(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{
Cell["FluxModel", "ObjectName"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   ButtonBox["FluxModel",
    BaseStyle->"Link",
    ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/FluxModel"], "[", "f", 
   "]"}]], "InlineFormula"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "is a flux for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["OsculatingInspiral",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/OsculatingInspiral"]], 
  "InlineFormula"],
 ": f[a, p, e, x] gives the orbit-averaged rates {dp/dt, de/dt, dx/dt} with \
respect to coordinate time.",
 "\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   ButtonBox["FluxModel",
    BaseStyle->"Link",
    ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/FluxModel"], "[", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<name\>\"", ",", 
    RowBox[{"param", "\[Rule]", "value"}], ",", "\[Ellipsis]"}], "]"}]], 
  "InlineFormula"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "is a built-in flux model.",
 "\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   ButtonBox["FluxModel",
    BaseStyle->"Link",
    ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/FluxModel"], "[", 
   "force", "]"}]], "InlineFormula"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "is the orbit average of the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["ForceModel",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/ForceModel"]], 
  "InlineFormula"],
 " force.",
 "\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   ButtonBox["FluxModel",
    BaseStyle->"Link",
    ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/FluxModel"], "[", 
   RowBox[{"model", ",", 
    RowBox[{"param", "\[Rule]", "value"}], ",", "\[Ellipsis]"}], "]"}]], 
  "InlineFormula"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "changes the parameters of a model.",
 "\n",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"model", "[", 
   RowBox[{"a", ",", "p", ",", "e", ",", "x"}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula"],
 "\[LineSeparator]",
 "gives the rates."
}], "Usage"],

Cell["\<\
A flux changes only the slowly varying elements p, e and x; the phases \
advance at their geodesic rates, plus the effect of any force. Forces and \
fluxes can be combined.\
\>", "Notes"],

Cell["\<\
With the option \"Rates\" -> {\"En\", \"L\", \"Q\"}, f gives the rates of the \
energy, angular momentum and Carter constant instead, and they are converted \
to those of p, e and x.\
\>", "Notes"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A physical flux has de/dt \[Proportional] e near e = 0, and dx/dt \
\[Proportional] 1 - x^2 near x = \[PlusMinus]1. A flux keeps a circular orbit \
circular. On an equatorial orbit, x = \[PlusMinus]1, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["OsculatingInspiral",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/OsculatingInspiral"]], 
  "InlineFormula"],
 " keeps the orbit in the plane, so the flux must not change x there."
}], "Notes"],

Cell["\<\
Fluxes are averages with respect to coordinate time, so an inspiral with a \
flux uses coordinate time.\
\>", "Notes"],

Cell["\<\
FluxModel[force] averages the force's rates of En, L and Q over the geodesic \
with respect to t, so the force must depend on neither \[Phi] nor t. Its \
parameter \"Points\" (16 by default) is the number of points in each phase; \
the average converges quickly, but is slow to evaluate.\
\>", "Notes"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The options are those of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["ForceModel",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/ForceModel"]], 
  "InlineFormula"],
 ", and \"Rates\". The flux is multiplied by the mass ratio \[Epsilon] unless \
\"ScalesWithMassRatio\" is False."
}], "Notes"],

Cell["\<\
The built-in model \"Peters\" gives the leading-order gravitational-wave \
rates of p and e (Peters 1964). It is Newtonian: it does not depend on the \
spin, and leaves x unchanged.\
\>", "Notes"],

Cell["Tech Notes", "TechNotesSection"],

Cell[TextData[ButtonBox["Osculating Orbital Elements",
 BaseStyle->"Link",
 ButtonData->
  "paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/tutorial/OsculatingOrbitalElements"]], \
"Tutorials"],

Cell["See Also", "SeeAlsoSection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["OsculatingInspiral",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/OsculatingInspiral"]], 
  "InlineSeeAlsoFunction"],
 " \[FilledVerySmallSquare] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["ForceModel",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:OsculatingOrbitalElements/ref/ForceModel"]], 
  "InlineSeeAlsoFunction"]
}], "SeeAlso"],

